
5s-Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
======================================


Date: 5 Nov 2009


When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:

K.G. Dyall, J. Phys. Chem. A (2009) 113:12638. Available from the Dirac web site, 
http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF orbital coefficients, given
with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the basis sets described in the above article. 

NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 

The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:

* Optimization of the s, p, and d sets separately, on the total energy of the 
  ground state
* Optimization of 3 functions for the 5p of Rb on the 5p eigenvalues of the 5p1
  configuration, followed by reoptimization on the total energy of the 5p1 
  configuration of the functions for the 3p-4p outer antinodes with the 5p 
  functions included and held fixed
* Optimization of 3 functions for the 5p of Sr along with reoptimization of the 
  functions for the 3p-4p outer antinodes, on the total energy of the 5p2
  configuration
* Replacement of the outermost d with the two 4p-correlating d functions and
  reoptimization of the remainder.

In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF 
exponent set. 

The correlating functions were generated as follows

* Functions for the 4s4p and 3d shells were optimized in SDCI calculations with
  excitations out of these shells coupled to J=0. Only the higher angular
  functions were included in the correlating space (2d1f and 2f1g).
* The correlating functions for the 5s in Sr (2d1f) were optimized in MR-SDCI
  calculations with the 5s and 5p orbitals in the active space. The inner d
  function of this set was very similar to the outer 4p-correlating d function, 
  and so the former was discarded. The outer d function of this set was then
  reoptimized with the outer 4p-correlating d function included and held fixed.
* The d function for the 5s in Rb was taken as 7/3 times the 2nd s exponent of
  the SCF set; this ratio matches the mean radii of the functions.
  
The functions for 4p dipole polarization were generated as follows:

* A polarizing s function was optimized on the polarizability of the 4p shell
  with 4p -> ns excitations in the perturbing space. The uncontracted SCF s
  functions (see below) for the 4s were included and their exponents were held 
  fixed. 
* The ratio of the dipole polarizing s exponent to the outer SCF s exponent for
  the 4s (4th exponent) was applied to the outer p exponent for the 4p (4th
  exponent) to generate a p function.
* A polarizing d function was optimized on the polarizability of the 4p shell
  with 4p -> nd excitations in the perturbing space. The 4p correlating d
  functions were included and their exponents were held fixed. 
* The ratio of the dipole polarizing d exponent to the outer correlating d 
  exponent was applied to the correlating f exponent for the 4p to generate an f
  function.

The contractions are taken from SCF calculations on a weighted average of the
5s^n and 5p^n configurations, with a weight ratio of 4:1.

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
   of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
   component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
   symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
   those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2.

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of 5s5p correlating functions.

4. Exponents of 4s4p correlating functions.

5. Exponents of 3d correlating functions.

6. Exponents of 4s4p polarizing functions


Basis set construction.
----------------------

For the s elements, the outer core (n-1)s and (n-1)p must be included in the
valence.  For Sr, the valence np is considered an occupied orbital and is
correlated; hence there are extra correlating functions for Sr.

Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
functions to the SCF set. These basis sets are described below.

NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 

1. Valence and outer core. To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
   1st, 3rd, 4th, and 6th s primitives from the SCF set
   1st, 3rd, 4th, and 6th p primitives from the SCF set
   4s4p correlating 2d1f set (includes 1st and 2nd d primitives from the SCF set)
   5s5p correlating set (1d for Rb, 1d1f for Sr)

   (HF + 4s4p3d1f/2f valence/outer core)


2. Core 3d correlation. To basis set 1, add the following:
   7th and 9th s primitives from the SCF set
   7th and 8th p primitives from the SCF set
   3rd and 4th d primitives from the SCF set
   3d correlating 2f1g set

   (HF + 4s4p3d1f/2f valence/outer core + 2s2p2d2f1g core)


3. Outer core (4s4p) polarization. To any of the above basis sets, add the 
   s, p, d, and f polarizing functions. The p polarizing function could be omitted,
   since the p gap is fairly small. 





